**Default in force: Book 8 follows the locked Book 7 — a tall man** — as the edition followed its own earlier books in #1 (Pellin) and #7 (Bracken). No locked text changes and no protected text changes.
**Option:** restore the source's tall woman by changing those five places in Book 7 (about ten words) and re-binding Book 7 (new hashes, a reissued DIRECTOR_CUT_READY, a republish), then reverting the Book 8 plan marks. Needed before Book 8 Movement 2 gives this person a pronoun; later is possible but costs more to undo.
Also settled from the same audit, without owner action: Teague CAN read (Book 7's protected line has him read a slip twice; Book 8's plan said he could not); the stroke and bar is on stakes thirty-two and thirty-four only; the court is about fifty cords across, not 159 (159 of Book 7's cords would be about 3,500 yards, against the plan's own 1,107).

> **Update 2026-10-11 (Book 8 Movement 2, #59):** from chapter 7 the edition gives Teague's spear-carrier "he/his" on the page (ch7:215, ch8:7/37/87/127, ch9:91), following the default (a tall man, as in locked Book 7). Choosing "the tall woman" now means re-touching those Book 8 lines as well as re-binding Book 7.

