   139	He set out seven bowls. He had laid six for a hundred nights, and taken one back; then seven, in a loft, with one that stood empty. Tonight there was a person for each, and he went along the row twice with his lips moving, like a man who distrusts a sum that has come out even.
   140	
   141	"That's wrong," he said. "There ought to be one over."
   142	
   143	"There's the mule," said Lira.
   144	
   145	"She's a bowl of her own. I filled it." He sat down heavily. "I'd got used to there being somebody who wasn't here."
   146	
   147	The healer ate out of the seventh, and she ate it sitting, on a stone, with the brown mule's rope under her boot. Cael did not look at that for long. She had her left hand spread on her knee, and between one spoonful and the next a thumb went down, and then a finger; her lips moved, and the hand opened again, and she began the count afresh.
   148	
   149	He knew what road lay a day ahead of them, and so did four others at that fire.
   150	
   151	Nobody asked her; he had written in a book, at a mill, that he would not, and Brom, who had opened his mouth, looked at that hand and shut it again and took up his spoon.
   152	
   153	It was Teague's boy who came across.
   154	
   155	He came round the end of the bank with a heel of bread in his fist, and stopped where the light of the second fire began, as a person stops at the edge of somebody else's yard. He was all wrists and collar. He looked back once, to where a captain sat with his arm along a board, and then he looked at Lira.
   156	
   157	"What's it like?" he said. "When it goes."
   158	
   159	She did not pretend that she had not understood him.
   160	
   161	"You saw. You were on that rim."
   162	
   163	"I saw your back. It went up." He had gone red, and he kept on. "I've had mine since I was fourteen. I've never been without it for the time it takes to sneeze. They say that in there it's like being a child again."
   164	
   165	"Who says?"
   166	
   167	"Carters."
   168	
   169	"Carters have never been in." She set her bowl down. "It's like the last step of a stair in the dark, when you thought that there was one more. Your foot goes through where it ought to be. That's all it is; the next stride's only grass."
   170	
   171	"Does it come back?"
   172	
   173	"At the same stride, going out. All of it, at once. It's never yet come back short."
   174	
   175	He chewed on that, and on the bread.
   176	
   177	"I'd want to know that I could stand it," he said. "Before it was asked of me."
   178	
   179	"Then ask him for a quiet day and a line with nobody on the rim. Don't find out on a morning when somebody's shooting at you."
   180	
   181	He nodded, with his whole head, as a horse nods; and he was back at his own fire before the man with the board had turned to look for him.
   182	
   183	Across the gravel a stick had been laid down. The old man sat with his leg out along a blanket, and the lean old woman, going by with her kettle, stopped and looked at two laths and a healer's linen as a joiner looks at another shop's door. She said nothing to him, and went on; and he inclined his head to her back, gravely, by about the width of a finger, as one office acknowledges a paper from another.
   184	
   185	Cael opened the book against his knee.
   186	
   187	*Two hundred and ninety-ninth day. A beck under the ridge's foot, a mile short of the ring. A hundred and twenty-seven. Vastin: twenty of thirty, and walking. The Stair's rim at noon. Karis laid the kit's cord on the dead man's figure, from the old iron to the floor: nine lengths and a piece. It is what it was. I did not touch it. A hundred and thirty-one days.*
   188	
   189	*Teague had me read the brow. Ground is a sum of what a man wants. I got two places of four.*
   190	
   191	*Known: her stone was gone from between the irons, and a turf laid where it had been. Somebody has put the place back as his sheet has it, since the thaw.*
   192	
   193	*Deployment since the Line: thirty-two.*
   194	
