   196	
   197	The road swung south at noon on the three hundredth day, and the line did not go with it.
   198	
   199	He had seen the place once, in a soft grey morning, with gnats over the pools. There was the hold's smoke, a mile off down the right hand, going straight up. There were two old ruts that kept on eastward into the low ground, with turf in them. He had not marked what stood between, since nobody in that file had then had any need of it.
   200	
   201	It was a cairn. Somebody had built it at the bend, as high as a man's hip, of flat stones laid with care; and from its foot a third way went off, north, up the side of the hill. It was no wider than a mule, and it climbed aslant through the pale grass to a saddle, and went over. Nothing with wheels had ever gone that way, and by the look of the turf not a great deal on legs; it was a road that was kept open by one person's feet, once in a round, and by whoever came down it to fetch her.
   202	
   203	Teague's hand came up flat, and the file stood.
   204	
   205	"Skins," he said. "There's a gate down there, and there's none for three days after. Mine have their own purse."
   206	
   207	Brom went, with the boy a length behind him and both crews' skins slung empty between them. Cael watched them grow small on the south road. He was a long while turning round.
   208	
   209	She had stopped the mule.
   210	
   211	She stood at the cairn's foot with the rope over her shoulder, a stride and a half from the nearest of them, as she stood everywhere. Her face was toward the path. He had seen her look so at a notch with frost in it, on the hundred and twenty-second day, with a grey jenny's breath on his neck; and that time she had clicked her tongue, and gone.
   212	
   213	She looked up it once.
   214	
   215	It was a straight look, and she did not hurry over it; it went up the hill to the saddle, as a person's eyes go along a bench in the morning to see how many are waiting. There were three gates beyond that saddle, each with its own bench and nobody on it who could mend a child.
   216	
   217	Then she turned her back on it and tightened a strap that did not want tightening.
   218	
   219	Lira was looking at the grass, and Karis had her notebook half out of her coat, and pushed it in again. Vastin, a stride behind the place where Brom had been, stood with both hands on the head of his stick, and his eyes were on a pannier.
   220	
   221	Cael did not go to her. He stood where a captain had stood him, and found that it was harder to watch a person stay than it had been to watch her go, since the staying had a price upon it, and nobody among the twelve but herself could read the figure.
   222	
   223	It was a long quarter of an hour before two figures came back up the south road.
   224	
   225	Brom had a sack on his right shoulder, and four skins swung full from his left hand, two of them new; he could carry so, now, and he carried as though somebody had given him back a whole second man. The boy had his own crew's, and a side of bacon that was no business of the six.
   226	
   227	"Four," said Brom. "Meal, and a cheese, and corn for her, and the two skins." He set the sack down. "A hundred and twenty-three. And I never named a thing. He'd the skins filled and standing in the gate, and a price on them."
   228	
   229	"He saw us from his wall," said Lira.
   230	
   231	"He'd had them standing since yesterday. A man rode through, two days gone, and told him there'd be a crew for the dry road; he bought nothing, and he gave no name. He only said." Brom looked at Seln. "I asked what he rode. It was a bay."
   232	
   233	Seln said nothing at all. He looked east.
   234	
   235	The healer had the mule's rope in her fist. She clicked her tongue, and walked on along the old ruts, past a cairn, at the one pace that she had.
   236	
   237	She gave it to them a hundred yards on, over her shoulder, since they would not ask.
   238	
   239	"I shut that shoulder on the two hundred and ninety-eighth. A mend that deep wants a week of somebody's eye, and I've said in a yard what I think of a healer who spends herself on a thing and walks off while it's deciding. That's the three hundred and fifth." Her hand had come up, and a thumb went down. "There's a knee behind it that's on my linen and twenty-one days old. And there's an edge at the end of that brass that's twice any I've stood at, with ten of you going at it. I said in a loft where somebody ought to be."
   240	
   241	"And the three?" said Brom.
   242	
