   244	
   245	She walked four strides.
   246	
   247	"That's a sum. Don't any of you dress it."
   248	
   249	---
   250	
   251	The track went under the turf in the last hour of the light.
   252	
   253	They had walked it hard. A tree as thick as Brom's leg stood in one rut, and it had put on a hand of girth; there were the pools on either side, black and still, and the silver-backed leaves coming. Five people and a healer had taken a day and a morning over that track once, in a strip a mile wide, with a hide held open and a man sent a furlong ahead to be sighted on. Twelve went along it now between noon and dusk, since eleven of them had only to follow a quiet man who knew where it came out.
   254	
   255	Twice the stick fell behind, and twice a flat hand went up for nothing that Cael could see; the file stood, and drank, and the stick came up. Nobody remarked on it. A captain who had said plainly that he would not walk ten at the pace of one would stop ten for a hare, it appeared, sooner than let one of them be seen to be waited for.
   256	
   257	Then there were no ruts, and the ground rose a little, and Seln was standing on the crown of it.
   258	
   259	The basin lay below them as round as a plate. The reeds in it were green at the foot now, and old straw above; and every one of them stopped at the one edge, on every side, as though a person had gone round with shears. A pale straight path crossed them. Four grey walls stood in the water at the end of it, roofless, with the evening in them.
   260	
   261	He heard the boy's breath go in, behind him.
   262	
   263	The four from the house on the rise had seen that place once, from where they stood now. They had found six pairs of breeches steaming on a rope, and a very big man lying strapped. Teague looked at it a good while.
   264	
   265	Vastin had come up last. He had stood a stride behind Brom on the Stair's brow, the day before, and looked down at a grey floor for the whole of an hour without a word; and Cael had thought then that a person who has read six years of sheets upon a place might have little left to say of the place itself. He stopped now at the rise's crown, on the stick, and his eyes went round the edge of the reeds, slowly, the whole way, as they would have gone round a seal.
   266	
   267	"It is at that edge," he said. "Where they stop."
   268	
   269	"To a boot's width," said Lira. "You could walk it with your eyes shut."
   270	
   271	"I have put my name to a good many papers which said that such a thing could not be. Not one of them was drawn by a person who had stood here." He considered the hall for a breath longer. "I find that I am glad to have been spared the sight until I had left off signing."
   272	
   273	"We'll not go down to the reeds," said Teague. "Nor will the beast. It's the track's end, on the grass, and nobody drinks what stands in that."
   274	
   275	They made their two fires a few yards outside the grey stone.
   276	
