    90	
    91	There were people at the timbers.
    92	
    93	Cael counted them. He had seen their like on a rim once, and in a stepped alley under a wall, and each time they had been six. There were seven. They stood in a row behind the upper baulk, and every one of them had a hood up and a cloth across the face, and every hood was turned toward the brow.
    94	
    95	They had not run to that barricade. They had been standing at it.
    96	
    97	"They knew," said Karis, under her breath.
    98	
    99	"That iron's been in the ground years. Not one cold term."
   100	
   101	It was Seln, and he had said it without turning from the fence.
   102	
   103	"I told you on this turf that it was a cordon, and that they had been here more than once. I had forty bars by one lamp when I said it. There is light enough tonight to see a quarter of the round." His voice was as mild as ever. "Some of that iron is bright, and a good deal of it is not; and a path has been trodden along the outside of it, the whole way, by people who walk it. Nobody wore that to the stone since the thaw, to meet twelve strangers. It was there for whoever came."
   104	
   105	"Two hundred and six," she said. "From the first sheet."
   106	
   107	Down on the strip, a point of white light stood up over the timbers, and hung there, and went out.
   108	
   109	"That's one with a Path," said Teague. "He's telling me that he's got it." He had not taken his eyes from the place. "Two with bows, on the top baulk; you can see the stocks. Four with staves. And him."
   110	
   111	He turned round, and looked at the eleven of them, and at a mule.
   112	
   113	"Back, behind the brow. There'll be no fire."
