   107	He went over it once more, as he had gone over it a dozen times already. He had been ten strides from those timbers, with one bow in front of him and five people standing on the grass at the back of it. They had gone over that iron because he looked at it. One of them was his. If seven hoods had seen a captain turn his back, with his arm hanging, to run and look into a hole, they would have seen a crew with its head off; and they had four staves and a great deal less to lose than he had supposed. He had heard a man whom he had walked beside for a great many cold terms go off a ledge. He had put his foot down.
   108	
   109	He would do it again. He knew that as well, and it did not help.
   110	
   111	"I'd made a rule about ledges," he said. "In an inn, before we walked. I'd told myself that a man who turns back on one for somebody who isn't his gets his own killed. I thought that I'd have to keep it against one of theirs." He looked at the linen. "I never priced keeping it against you."
   112	
   113	The bow breathed for a little.
   114	
   115	"If you'd turned, I'd have had something to say to you from the bottom of that hole," he said. "I'd a deal of breath for it, till I landed." His mouth worked. "It's the good one. That's what I can't get past. I've favoured the other for more cold terms than I'll own to, and it's sound as a bell, and I've gone and broken the one that I was favouring it with."
   116	
   117	"She says it's knit."
   118	
   119	"She says it's knit, and that it'll carry me on a stick tomorrow if I'm not a fool. I've never seen anything like it. She put her hands round it, and I could feel it being done." He turned his head on the bracken, and his voice dropped. "She asked me first. With my leg lying two ways. She'd not lay a finger on it until I'd said."
   120	
   121	"I saw."
   122	
   123	"I've had a bone set by that one for half my life," said the bow, with his chin toward the turf wall, "and she's never once asked me anything but whether I'd finished squealing."
   124	
   125	The old woman, who was asleep, said nothing.
   126	
   127	The boy had not stirred. He had gone down as a felled thing goes down, an hour since, in the corner by the door, sitting, with his head against the turf and his mouth open. He had run the length of a shelf and down a fall of rock. He had held a man's head off the stones for a quarter of an hour without being told to. He had carried water down, and the board's webbing, and a healer's pannier, and had come up under one corner of a blanket. Then he had sat down to wait until he was wanted, and it had taken him like a blow.
   128	
   129	The cloth was in his fist. It was dry. He had got it out for a wrist, Teague supposed, and found that there was an old woman at the wrist already, and had not known what else to do with it.
   130	
   131	---
   132	
   133	Teague looked at him for a good while.
   134	
   135	He had looked at him on the shelf, across a row of iron, and the boy had gone over. That was the whole of what had been done. No word had been given. A captain had turned his head and let his eyes rest; and a boy had lifted his foot as high as a child's and put it down in a place where, by everything that he had ever been told, a person left off being what he was.
   136	
   137	*Four,* he had said, in there. Teague had heard it from the shale.
   138	
   139	He had counted a boy as a man in an inn, to stop him fretting on a stair. He had not thought much about what the boy would do with it. He knew now. He would go wherever he was looked at, a half-beat before he had seen what was there, and he would say the count as he went, so that nobody should forget that he was on it.
   140	
   141	That was a thing to know about one of one's own. It was a thing to be a good deal more careful with than Teague had been this morning.
   142	
   143	He had held, though, at the edge of that grass. The bow had gone off a ledge thirty feet from him, and he had stood. Teague had seen that too, out of the tail of his eye, and had not had the leisure to wonder at it. There had been no hand on him. There had been a girl's boots.
   144	
   145	He would have to think about whose boots a boy watched.
   146	
   147	The other boy, the one with the book, he did not have to think about, since he had been within a stride of him for the only three breaths that mattered.
   148	
   149	He had asked for a left side kept, with a stick, and he had been given exactly that. A stave had come at his own bent head, and it had been met. Another had come across at the boy's throat, and the boy had put a stick up on end and taken the rest on his neck, and had not given back the length of a boot. Teague had felt the place stay full behind his shoulder. A man who is setting a line can feel that, as he feels a floor.
   150	
   151	And that was all that the boy had done.
   152	
   153	Teague had seen him do other things. He had stood on a ridge at dusk and watched a Wind's crack, and a fire, and something that went up a ditch like a squall, all out of one small shape with its arm raised. He did not know what that one carried; he had left off supposing that he would be told. But whatever it was, it had been within a yard of a stave this morning, and it had stayed where it was kept.
   154	
   155	*Nobody declares.* The boy had asked that of another man's field, and then he had paid for it himself, in bone, with a stick out of a town.
   156	
   157	Teague could put that in a column. It was the first thing about that one which he ever had been able to.
   158	
   159	He got up, since he could not sit any longer with it. The arm came with him like a parcel. He went out past the blanket, and round the hut to the ravine's side, where a hide lay over something with stones along its edge.
   160	
   161	He had had the spear lift a corner of it, in the forenoon, and had looked, and had let it fall. He lifted it himself now, left-handed.
   162	
   163	There was a sack of meal, and half of another. There was a bundle of bolts in a thong, with never a thread of colour on their feathers. There was a mallet with an iron head, and a coil of tarred cord no thicker than a pencil, with a knot in it at every few feet.
   164	
   165	And there were the bars.
   166	
   167	They lay in a bundle, a dozen of them, tied with a strip of hide: square iron as thick as two fingers, drawn to a point, and never driven. They were new. There was not so much as a bloom of rust upon one. He turned the nearest to the light with his boot, and read what was struck beneath its head, since he could read a stamp as well as a slip.
   168	
   169	It was two figures, and under the two figures a short stroke with a bar across its foot.
   170	
   171	He knew the mark. He had seen it in pencil on a sheet of paper, in a back room, with a small pale woman's finger beside it; he had gathered that it was a grade of some kind, and had to do with how old a thing was. He had not come for marks. He had said so, at her door.
   172	
   173	But he stood over a bundle of bright iron for some time, with a dead arm, on a shelf that seven people had been paid to keep; and he thought that whoever sent a dozen new bars to the far end of the country, with a mallet to drive them and seven people to do the driving, was not a person who had forgotten that the place was here.
   174	
   175	He let the hide fall.
   176	
   177	It was not his to read. He had given the reading away in front of fifty people, and he would not take it back for a thing under a hide. He would tell the pale one what lay there, when she came up from that bridge, and she would make of it what a paper made.
   178	
   179	He went back to count his four.
   180	
   181	---
   182	
   183	They went down to it at noon, when the sun stood over the dish and nothing in it had a shadow.
   184	
   185	There were six. Karis had the hide and a roll of paper, and Lira the pebbles. Brom came, since he said that he had not walked five days to look at a bridge from a hill, and Seln, who said nothing. Vastin had got up from the upper baulk when he saw them go to the iron, and had taken his place a stride behind Brom without being asked.
   186	
   187	The healer was at the bars before them, with her sleeves pushed up.
   188	
   189	"I said that I'd be here for this, on a morning when nobody was shooting," she said. "It's noon, and I'll take it. Wrist. May I?"
   190	
   191	"Aye," said Brom.
   192	
   193	She put two fingers on it, and he stepped over. Cael watched her lips. They moved, and they stopped, as he had seen them stop at a stone above a stair and at a kerb in a marsh.
   194	
   195	"Four," said Oryn. "The same."
   196	
   197	She drew him back by the wrist, and the count went on past four, and she let him go.
   198	
   199	"It's twice the size and it does the one thing. That's all that I wanted, and I'll want it again when you come out." She looked at the six of them, and last at Cael. He had a figure ready for her, and had been turning over how to give it with an old man standing five feet off. She saw that he had something. She shook her head. "Not at a fence. I said that I'd not ask how many, and I'll not be told across a row of iron either."
   200	
   201	They went down the turf.
   202	
   203	It was four hundred and forty yards, and two strides. He had seen the figure written by lamplight, on a night when nobody would cross. It was a long way to walk with nothing on either hand, and the grey thing grew as they came, and he could not make it stop growing.
   204	
   205	Then they were at the foot of it.
   206	
   207	The ravine came in from their left, under the turf's edge, and ran on before them; it was deeper here than at the shelf, and its walls were the same dark rock. Two piers stood up out of it. They were of the grey. He knew that grey as he knew the colour of his own hands. There was no course in them, and no join; they rose out of the rock of either wall as the stones on the Fallow rose out of their floor, and the arch sprang from the one to the other in a single piece.
   208	
   209	There was a road on its back.
   210	
   211	It was the thing which nobody had been able to make, from the brow. The grey came up out of the turf at their feet, a cart wide, with no kerb to it and no step, and went on upward as a road goes up a hill; it rose over the piers to a crown, and went down the other side, and into the grass again. A person might have driven a loaded wain across it. There was not the mark of a wheel anywhere upon it.
   212	
   213	"No stair," said Brom. "The last one was all stair."
   214	
   215	"It is not for going down," said Karis. She had stopped with one boot on the grey. "It is for going over."
   216	
   217	Behind his eyes the folded thing had come about.
   218	
   219	Cael had been waiting for that. It had leaned at a bridge once before, and at a plank, and at a hinge; it leaned now at the whole of this, with a steadiness that he had never had from it, like a head turned toward the only voice in a room. He gave his eyes the turf. He counted his own bootlaces, twice. It came up short at last, and lay down, and he did not think that it had gone far.
   220	
   221	He walked up the grey.
   222	
   223	It took him forty strides. The wind met him as he rose, and the dish opened round him, and he came to the place where the road stopped climbing.
   224	
   225	He stood on the crown.
   226	
   227	The middle of such a place was what he had wanted, ever since a small pale woman said three words on a road. He had said so on his feet, at a fire, to six people. This was the middle of the fourth: the centre of a ring half a mile across, which an office had held a sheet upon for two hundred and six years and had fenced with iron and left seven people to walk round.
   228	
   229	Nothing happened.
   230	
   231	There was the wind. There were the things that he carried, each in its place, as they were in a meadow: a held breath in his soles, and a hot grain, and a thin note; a post, and a rule folded on a shelf. Under his breastbone they went round what they had always gone round, and he did not go to look.
   232	
   233	There was no bird over that ground, and no beast on it.
   234	
   235	Only the newest of them had altered. It lay along him, from the heel to the hand, and it was listening to what he stood on.
   236	
   237	He understood why. A man had tried to drive a stake into this grey once, under a floor, and had struck twice, and had laid his mallet down. Nothing went into it. Of everything that a person might brace a heel against in all that country, there was not a thing so hard; and the prop that lay along him knew it, as a mason's hand knows a sound stone. It had found one end of a line, the best that it would ever be offered.
   238	
   239	The old three ribs ached, low on the left, in a row.
   240	
   241	He had it then, standing in the wind. What a push cost was paid upon the road that it had gone by, every time, at the place where that road left the body. It came out of a captain at the wrist, since that was where that man was weakest. The thing knew where it would come out of him.
   242	
   243	"Well?" said Lira, from the foot of the road.
   244	
   245	"Nothing," said Cael. "Grass, and a view."
   246	
   247	---
   248	
   249	It was Lira who found the notch.
   250	
   251	She had come up beside him, on the crown, and stood with her hand over her eyes; and she was not looking at the dish, as he had been; she was looking along the grey, the way that it ran, over the far fence and out.
   252	
   253	"There," she said. "On the line. Past the second ridge."
   254	
   255	He followed her finger, to where the hills stood up beyond the dish, blue behind brown, in the soft uneven rows that hills make; and in one of those rows there was a gap, low down.
   256	
   257	"It's square," said Lira. "Hills aren't. Look at the foot of it."
   258	
   259	"A cut?"
   260	
   261	"Or a place that's been used for one. I can't tell at that distance, and I'd be guessing." She let her hand fall. "The brass goes through it. I'd lay my knife."
   262	
   263	Seln had come up on Cael's other side without a sound. He looked at that gap for longer than either of them had, and his face told nothing.
   264	
   265	They went down under the arch by a rib of rock on the western side.
   266	
   267	It was cool in the ravine's bed, and dry; the two piers went up on either hand like the legs of a table, and between them the daylight came down grey off the underside of the road. Twenty yards on, where the bed ended, there was a hole.
   268	
   269	It was round, and as wide as a cart is long, and the rock went into it smooth, as the sides of a funnel go, and there was no bottom that he could see. Brom picked up a stone as big as his fist, and looked at Karis, and put it down again.
   270	
   271	"That is where it goes," she said. "Whatever falls on this country. I shall not drop anything into it; I do not know what is under."
   272	
   273	She had turned from it already, to the western pier.
   274	
   275	Its inner face was cut.
   276	
   277	He had known that it would be. The sunk lines ran in their courses from the bed upward, five to the height of a man, straight and hooked and crossing, as close as the words on a page; they were as sharp as on the day that they were made, since no foot had ever worn them and no rain had reached them there. She stood before that face with her gloves off, and for the space of a dozen breaths she did not move.
   278	
   279	"It is the same hand," she said. "And I can read it." Her voice shook a little, and she steadied it. "I stood in front of four walls of this under a floor, with six hours of oil, and I could not have told you that they were letters. Sheet."
   280	
   281	Lira had it against her fingers before the word was done.
   282	
   283	They were two hours in that place. Karis did not rub; she drew, with the paper pinned to a board of Brom's, sign after sign, and a small tick wherever a run ended, as a man under a town had taught her. Cael had no lamp to hold. She gave him the other thing, which was to count.
   284	
   285	"The lowest course, on this face. The signs in it, from the corner to the corner. I want it against my drawing."
   286	
   287	He counted, going along the stone with his finger held a hair off it, as he would have gone along a row of figures, and at the far corner he had fifty-one.
   288	
   289	"Fifty-two," said Karis, without looking up.
   290	
   291	"I've fifty-one."
   292	
   293	"Then one of us has missed a sign. Do it again."
   294	
   295	He did it again, from the corner, more slowly, and he had fifty-one.
   296	
   297	He stood and looked at the stone: a row of cut marks, in good light, each a hand's breadth from the next. He had counted the heads of a herd at a furlong, two days since, and had not been out by one.
   298	
   299	"Lira," he said.
   300	
   301	She came, and put her own finger to the corner, and they went along the course together, he saying the figures and she moving her lips.
   302	
   303	"Fifty-two," said Lira.
   304	
   305	"Fifty-two," said Cael.
   306	
   307	He had had it that time; he could not have said which sign his eye had gone over twice before, since they all stood there now, as plain as fence posts.
   308	
   309	Karis had lifted her head. She looked at him for the space of a breath, and then at the course, and she did not say anything at all. She had measured an inn yard once at forty-two strides in four people's hands, and at forty-one and a piece in his. He saw her remember it.
   310	
   311	"It was fifty-two," he said. "I'll enter that I made it less, twice. I'll not enter why, since I don't know."
   312	
   313	"That is how I should enter it," said Karis.
   314	
   315	Behind his eyes the folded thing had leaned again, and he had let his finger go on along the stone and given his eyes the bed of the ravine; but he had not been quick enough. It had had the lowest course for the length of a breath. It gave him what it gave of such things, flatly, with no voice in it.
   316	
   317	*Goes.*
   318	
   319	It went eastward, and he would have known which way without being told. Every run of that course leaned as the grey road leaned over his head: out from the western pier, across twelve yards of air, to the face of its fellow, and on.
   320	
   321	Karis had stopped drawing.
   322	
   323	She had the pencil's butt on a group of signs in the second course, at the height of her chin. It stood by itself, with a tick on either side of it, and she had gone back to it three times.
   324	
   325	"I have a word here that I can turn," she said. "It is the only one on this face that I am sure of."
   326	
   327	She read it off the stone, once, as she read a figure.
   328	
   329	"Through," said Karis.
   330	
   331	"So it's a gate," said Brom.
   332	
   333	"It is a word on a pier. I am telling you what the stone says, and where it stands. I shall want all of these on a blanket before I tell you anything more."
   334	
   335	---
   336	
   337	He wrote it by the watch's fire, at dusk, with eleven people about it and one in a hut.
   338	
   339	Karis had been told what lay under a hide. She had gone and looked, and had come back saying nothing.
   340	
   341	*Three hundred and fourth day. The watch's post, on the shelf above the fourth place. A hundred and twenty-three. Vastin: twenty-five; he stood inside the line this morning, and it took what he carries, and gave it back. Oryn is four days past her cairn. She knit a shin in the ravine's bed and was grey for a quarter of an hour.*
   342	
   343	Then he wrote the rest, and it came as the last had come, at a fork, all in one piece.
   344	
   345	*Twelfth—no, count it again—thirteenth. Wind, Pressure, Iron, Compression, Ember, Shadow, Storm, Anchor, Blade, Tide, Iron Wall, Rune, Force. Thirteen things that aren't a Path, and I got three of them in one city and one bridge. Rate's climbing and I'm writing that down because Hesk taught me to note the trend. The Span's the fourth site and the Compact fenced it, years ago by the scale on the iron, and left men on it. They knew. They've always known. The bearing runs through it and keeps going, oldest-last, and the oldest one's out there in the deep edge, quiet since they built the machine. One more site. I've wanted to stand in the middle of it since Karis said* someone made this. *Tomorrow I find out what standing in the middle of the first one feels like.*
   346	
   347	He read it over.
   348	
   349	He had written *tomorrow*, and it was three days, by an office's figure, and a little less; he had seen the divider's point come down on a clean sheet. He looked at the word for a while, and he let it stand. It was not a true count; it was the truth about how near the thing had come, and he had no other word for that.
   350	
   351	He set the count beneath it, since a count was owed.
   352	
   353	*The thirteenth came at the timbers, at a stride, from Teague. It is not used. He is not told. That is the second such debt, and it is entered beside the first.*
   354	
   355	*Nothing opened today: not on the shelf, and not over the iron. The read at the seep was the last. Deployment since the Line: thirty-three.*
   356	
   357	*A course on the west pier: fifty-two signs. I made it fifty-one, twice. Ruling: hold.*
   358	
   359	*I stood on the crown at noon. Nothing.*
   360	
   361	The fire had a ring round it by then, like a moon in mist. He had not turned the folded thing upon that pier; it had taken one course of cut stone for itself, between a breath and the next, and it was charging him for the look all the same. He shut the book, and put the heel of his hand to each eye in turn, and sat with his face to the dark until it should have done.

