     1	# Chapter 52 — One Blanket
     2	
     3	The tall man cut the second stick out of the watch's own trestle.
     4	
     5	He did it on the three hundred and fifth day, as soon as there was light, with the hand-axe from a captain's belt, while the rest were lashing loads. He took one leg off the frame that had held two timbers, and trimmed the foot of it square, and bound a pad of rag over the head. Then he carried it into the hut and stood it against the wall by the bracken, and came out again, and nothing was said by anybody.
     6	
     7	The broad man came through the blanket in the door a quarter of an hour later, on that leg of pine and an old woman's arm.
     8	
     9	He was grey, and his mouth was shut like a purse. A shin that had lain two ways the morning before took his weight, a little at a time, with a board bound along it. When he had gone the length of the shelf and back he shook the arm off, and stood by himself, and looked at the east as if it owed him money.
    10	
    11	They left a fire burning low, and a keg with three coppers on its lid.
    12	
    13	It was the trodden path that they took, round the outside of the iron. Six of them had walked down that dish to its middle the noon before, and Cael would have gone so again. But there were people in that file with something to lose at a stride, and a beast, and nobody proposed it. They went a quarter of the round with the bars at their right hand. On the far side Karis stopped the whole file for as long as it takes to lace a boot. She stood at a bar that was like every other, and looked back across the dish. From there the grey road on the bridge's back lay end-on to her, like a ruler seen along its edge; and when she turned about, the same line went out before her, over two ridges, to a square gap in the hills.
    14	
    15	She made a mark in her notebook, and the file went on.
    16	
    17	It was the slowest day's walking that they had done since a ford.
    18	
    19	Nobody had ruled that either. A captain with his arm in linen to the shoulder had lifted two fingers at the fence, and had set a pace with his own boots that a child could have kept. Cael, a stride behind that shoulder, understood that it had been taken from the hindmost and handed forward, man by man, without a word. There were two at the back now who went on wood.
    20	
    21	He could hear them. There was the old one, a stride behind Brom, where he had always been: the ash went down, and the stiff leg came through. And at the tail there was a new beat, heavier, with a grunt in it at every third stride, where a man who had walked last for a great many cold terms on a bad hip was learning to walk last on a pine trestle.
    22	
    23	They did not keep the same time. But at every halt the two stopped within a breath of one another, and each stood leaning on his stick with his eyes to the front, twenty yards apart. Neither turned his head toward the other by the width of a finger.
    24	
    25	Lira had seen it. So had Brom; Cael heard him let his breath out through his nose at the second halt, and knew what that noise was.
    26	
    27	The country changed before noon.
    28	
    29	The grass thinned, and then there was more stone than grass. It was shale, grey and dark, lying in great flat steps, each as broad as a field and no higher than a man, so that they went down the land as down a wide stair. There was no water anywhere on it, and nothing grew in the cracks but a thin red moss. The gap in the hills stood up ahead of them the whole day, square, and came no nearer than a thing does that is watched.
    30	
    31	Teague called the halt in the middle of the afternoon, under one of the steps.
    32	
    33	It was a good place, and Cael could see why it had been chosen, since he had been taught the sum. The step stood at their backs as high as a man's head. The flat below it ran out forty yards to the next lip. Nothing could come at it from behind, and anything that came from in front must cross the flat. The gap was four miles off, perhaps, or five.
    34	
    35	"That's the day," said Teague. "I'll not take two sticks nearer to that than this until somebody's been and looked."
    36	
    37	Seln had his coat buttoned already. He went away eastward over the lip, alone, with his hands empty, and in a little while there was nothing to be seen of him at all.
    38	
    39	---
    40	
    41	She asked for Brom's blanket, since it was the largest that they had. She meant to lay the whole line out upon it as it lay across the country.
    42	
    43	She had it spread on the shelf, twenty yards from where the boy was laying a fire, and upwind of it; she had never been willing to have a spark near paper. Then she stood at its edge with the hide under one arm and the courier's flat under the other, and looked at a grey square of wool as he had seen her look at a wall under a floor.
    44	
    45	"I have had these in three boxes for the better part of two hundred days," she said. "I have never had them in one place. I want everything that anybody has found on this, in order, before the light goes; and I should be glad if nobody put down a pebble that I had not asked for."
    46	
    47	She laid the hide in the middle.
    48	
    49	It went down under four stones, with the clean sheet pinned to its eastern edge by two thorns from a hedge at a ford. She turned it, a little, and a little more, with her eye on the gap in the hills, until the pencil line that ran through four rings ran also at that gap. The Ternhall brass went down upon the line.
    50	
    51	"That is how the country lies, and so everything else on this blanket will lie as it lay on the ground. I wrote on each sheet which wall it came from, and which way that wall faced. I did not know at the time what I wanted it for."
    52	
    53	The sheets came out of the middle box in their order.
    54	
    55	She did not lay all fifty-two. She took one from the Fallow, and set it at the hide's western end, beside the first ring; and she turned it about on the wool until its head pointed as a certain stone's had pointed, on a white floor, a long way behind them. Three from the Stair went down beside the second ring, each turned to its own wall. There was one from the hall, which a girl had held her by the belt to get. And beside the fourth ring, in three rows of three, she laid the nine that she had drawn in a ravine the day before, with the pencil hardly dry on them.
