     1	# Chapter 53 — The Shale Step
     2	
     3	There was wood for one fire.
     4	
     5	Teague's boy had carried it from the watch's own stack, a bundle of split pine on his back, since nothing on that shale would burn; and when he had laid it under the step, with a flint, there was plainly not enough of it to be divided. So the two crews sat down at one hearth for the first time on that road. Nobody had ruled it, and nobody remarked on it. They sat in two halves all the same, with a yard of stone between, as two households sit along a bench at a burying.
     6	
     7	Brom laid seven bowls. The boy, on his own crew's side, laid five.
     8	
     9	Brom looked at the row when it was done. It went the whole way round the fire, twelve bowls of two different turnings, and there was a person coming for each.
    10	
    11	"Twelve," he said. "And still none over."
    12	
    13	"There's the mule," said Lira.
    14	
    15	"She's had hers. She had it before any of you." He sat down. "I laid six for a hundred nights and took one back. I've never in my life wanted so much to see an empty bowl."
