   188	
   189	On her right was the notebook itself, open where it had lain open on a blanket. It showed her own second copy of a thing that she had read aloud once. There was an empty box ruled in its fifth line, where four or five letters had been scraped away, and one upright stroke was drawn at the box's left side.
   190	
   191	There was a hand's breadth of bare wood between the two.
   192	
   193	She had put that wood there on purpose. She had put the whole of a blanket there, by daylight, and a pebble at either end.
   194	
   195	She was not a fool. She had not carried that leaf the length of a blanket because she could see no line. An old man had lifted his hand, with a clerk's sheet in it, and had begun; and she had brought her own hand down before he could come to a tenth word, since she had known where it was going. She had known it before he opened his mouth. She had known it, if she was honest, for longer than that.
   196	
   197	There was a sentence.
   198	
   199	It existed. She would not pretend to herself that it did not. A thing does not cease to be there because a person has declined to look at it; she had learned that from a floor with a hole in it.
   200	
