   211	A measurement was a fact about a ruler. She had two rulers on that lid. The one had been laid upon every crossing that she had seen, by seven people, and it told her how a thing went out and came back. The other was a man's own account of himself, a long while dead, with his name scraped off it by a hand that she could not identify. Neither had been laid on the other. There was nothing on that lid, or in three boxes, or in a courier's flat, that would serve to lay them so.
