   115	"I shall tell you when the round is shut, and not before," said Karis.
   116	
   117	The sun went over, and the water in Brom's skin went down. The fourth station was behind them, and the fifth, and at each of the holes she had done a thing that he did not at first understand. She had made Lira and Seln draw one length of the cord dead straight between two points of the cut. Then she had laid the slip of ash across, at the second knot, from the taut hemp to the lip, and had marked the slip with her thumbnail.
   118	
   119	"What's that for?" Brom asked her, at the sixth.
   120	
   121	"It is how I shall know whether it is round. A straight cord laid across the edge of a round thing stands off from it in the middle. If the thing is truly round, it stands off by the same amount wherever I lay it." She held the slip up. There were six nail-marks on it, and he could not have told them apart. "A span and two fingers. I have never had six that agreed so."
   122	
   123	By the hundred and thirtieth his ribs had begun to tell him how far two miles is, and he walked with his left arm held in, and wrote with the book against his hip.
   124	
   125	The mark on the rim grew. It became a box, with a brown coat upon it, and an upright stick, and there were people about it now, and a mule. The old man had not shifted. Cael could see, at two hundred yards, that he was sitting exactly as he had been left, with both his hands on the ash and his face to the north, like a post that somebody has driven and gone away from.
   126	
   127	"A hundred and fifty-eight," said Karis.
   128	
   129	"A hundred and fifty-eight."
   130	
   131	There was not room for another length. Lira let the eye fall; she took the slip from Karis, and went on along the lip with it, laying it end over end, and saying the figures herself.
   132	
   133	She laid it nineteen times, and on the nineteenth its far end touched the foot of a length of ash.
   134	
   135	"Nineteen," said Lira, and sat down on the worn rock where she stood, and put her head on her knees.
   136	
   137	"A hundred and fifty-eight lengths, and nineteen strides," said Karis.
   138	
   139	"I've the same," said Cael. He turned the book to her, where there were thirty-one fives, and three strokes, and a nineteen written small.
   140	
