   162	
   163	She was a long time silent.
   164	
   165	"It is one hand," she said at last.
   166	
   167	"It's always one hand," said Lira. "You said so under the Stair."
   168	
   169	"I said so, and I was mistaken. I had four places, and at every one of them I saw the same forty-one kinds in the same order, cut alike, and I called it one hand." She did not look up. "Those are cut to the depth of my thumbnail. These are cut to the depth of my finger. And these were cut by somebody who was not looking at anything else while he did it."
   170	
   171	"How can you tell that?" said Brom.
   172	
   173	"He slipped."
   174	
   175	She pointed with the pencil's butt. Cael looked at the place, and gave his eyes nothing more than the place; it was a sign like a gate, in the second course, and from its lower corner a scratch ran out aslant, the length of a finger joint, where a tool had skidded on the grey.
   176	
   177	"That is an accident, and nobody means a thing like that. A hand went wrong, a very long time ago, and left it." She had two sheets on her knee already, which Lira had fetched out of the middle box at a word: one that she had drawn in a ravine three days before, and one from under a marsh. "It is on the west pier, in the second course. It is on this, from the hall. I drew it both times, and took it for part of the sign. It is cut there as carefully as everything else, to the same depth as everything else, at the same slant."
   178	
   179	Nobody spoke.
   180	
   181	"A person who makes a mark for himself does not make the slip a second time," said Karis. "A person who is copying, and has been told to leave nothing out, copies the slip. I have seen it in a clerk's hand at the foot of six sheets." She laid her palm flat on the notebook. "I have read strata since I was a girl. I have laid these floors under everything that I had. And there has been one more under them, all the time."
   182	
   183	Nobody else on that rock could have said the next thing; and for the space of a breath she did not say it either.
   184	
   185	"The others are copies," said Karis.
   186	
   187	She sat back on her heels. Her face was quite white, and Cael had seen it so only once before, in a gallery, on the morning when she said that she could read. He thought that being believed, which had lain so heavy on her beside a lamp two nights ago, could hardly have been heavier than this.
   188	
   189	She had the hide out a little later, on the flat top of a box, with the clean sheet pinned to its edge.
   190	
   191	"I said that I should ink this when I had had a cord round it." There was a ring on that paper which a thumbnail had made. She went round it with a pen, slowly, and blew on it. "Now it is a place."
   192	
   193	The Ternhall brass went down along the pencil line, through four rings, and ended a finger short of the join.
   194	
   195	"I shall want the other," she said. "It is lent, I know."
   196	
   197	He gave her Hesk's strip from its cloth, and did not touch the hide. She laid it down from the middle of the fourth ring to the middle of the fifth, by itself, without looking at the first; and then she slid it along its own length, a hair at a time, until its end met the end of the Ternhall.
   198	
   199	They met square, and there was not a thread of light between the two edges and the pencil.
   200	
   201	"Both," said Karis. "By two makers. I ruled it with the one at a grey stone, and I have checked it with the other at the end, and they agree."
   202	
   203	Cael looked at two strips of brass lying in one line across a calfskin. An old man had sent him the second, over a river, with a note about a thing that his grandmother used to say. He did not think of the note now so much as of the woman, whom he had never seen. She had been right, and she had not known the half of it, since somebody had drawn that line before either strip was rolled.
   204	
   205	"And past it?" said Lira.
   206	
   207	She had been up on the dish's eastern lip with Seln, and had come back.
   208	
   209	"Nothing. I looked till my eyes ran. There's no gap in anything, and no road, and not a bump. It's plain till it's sky."
   210	
   211	"There are eight peg-holes round this place, and no ninth beyond it," said Seln. "No path leaves it."
   212	
