   229	---
   230	
   231	Teague came down off his swell when the sun was a hand above the plain.
   232	
   233	"Well?" he said. "What's out there besides us?"
   234	
   235	"Nine people's marks leave the foot of the ridge where their path comes down, and go south," said Seln. "I followed them a mile this morning before you were awake. They did not come onto this rock. I have seen no smoke in any quarter since."
   236	
   237	"South. With three bows, and a day in hand."
   238	
   239	He said it quietly, and he was looking back the way that they had come, where a ridge stood small and brown against the west with one notch in it.
   240	
   241	Cael had known since the stream-bed that he would have to say it again. It had been easy behind a brow, when nobody had been hurt, and he had only been asking a man to spare seven strangers whom he had no cause to hate.
   242	
   243	He gave the captain the same three sentences that he had given him four nights before, in a fold of the ridge, in the same order; and he did not count the hoods afresh. Then he added one, since it was owed.
   244	
   245	"And if they're on that plain, they're let off it."
   246	
   247	The grey eyes came round to him.
   248	
   249	It was a long look, and there was a heap of stones in it, seven miles off, and a cloth, and a bowl with a chip in its rim.
   250	
   251	"I heard you the first time," said Teague. "I'd not have heard you yesterday at noon. I'll say that, so you know what you're asking." His left hand had closed on his belt. "It stands. I kept it on a shelf with him lying under me, and I'll not unkeep it on a flat."
   252	
   253	"You, with the hide. I'm asking. What is it?"
   254	
   255	"It is the fifth place, and it has two bars to its stroke, and its first sheet is older than any that I have a figure for." Karis spoke as she read a label. "I can tell you what four such places have done to seven people. They took what each carried, at a stride, and gave it back at a stride, whole. I cannot tell you that this one does as the four did. I should want one person over, and back, before a second went."
   256	
   257	"With my fingers on him," said Oryn.
   258	
   259	"It's past the line," said Teague. He looked at Cael. "You said how, at the fence. Say it here."
   260	
   261	"As it was there. One at a time, and each says what he finds."
   262	
   263	The light was going level. Out on the plain the rock had begun to turn the colour of ash, as rock does at that hour; and the floor had not, but lay under them, pale, and kept what it had been given.
   264	
   265	Lira was first.
   266	
   267	She stood at the cut with her wrist held out. The healer asked her two words, as she asked everybody, and was answered, and laid two fingers on it. Lira had done this at four places, and by night on one of them, nine times, with her foot bare on the frost. She lifted her boot now over a line two fingers wide as she would have stepped over a sill at home.
   268	
   269	Cael saw her shoulders go tight, and ease.
   270	
   271	"Four," said Oryn. Her lips had stopped. "The same."
   272	
   273	Lira brought the boot back.
   274	
   275	"Right."
   276	
   277	It was the word that she kept for a thing which had turned out to be exactly what it was.
   278	
   279	Brom went next, with his wrist asked for and held, and came to a stop a stride inside, beside the first stump. He did not come back at once; he looked down at the thing, and then he put his palm on the top of it.
