   340	
   341	He stepped over.
   342	
   343	Cael saw it take him. The heavy shoulders went back, as though somebody had drawn a pack off them from behind; and the left hand came up, slowly, and closed upon the right wrist, where there was linen, and a slat, and under those a joint that had paid for everything that he had ever done. He stood so for the length of three breaths.
   344	
   345	He stepped back.
   346	
   347	"My crew's stood at four of these. It's stood at enough," Teague said.
   348	
   349	He looked round then, as he had looked round on a shelf beside a row of iron, at the boy who stood where his left shoulder would have been.
   350	
   351	"Field's mine. This isn't field."
   352	
   353	It was the ruling that he had given at that fence, in two words, when he asked to be told how.
   354	
   355	He did not put his three across. Cael saw the tall man's eyes go to the cut, and to his captain; and the captain shook his head, by the width of a finger.
   356	
   357	Then Teague lifted two fingers of his left hand.
   358	
