     1	# Chapter 59 — Five Hundred and Six
     2	
     3	*One.*
     4	
     5	He counted, since he counted everything, and since a small pale woman had given him a figure to walk to.
     6	
     7	The first twenty strides were over the rows. He did not look down at them. He felt them through the soles of his boots, course after course, each cut as deep as a finger, as a person feels the boards of a bridge; and he kept his eyes upon a star that stood low in the east, a little to the left of where the far rim must be. He had chosen it before he lifted his foot, since there was nothing else out there to steer by.
     8	
     9	*Twenty.*
    10	
    11	Then the cutting stopped, and the grey was bare.
    12	
    13	He had not known how much noise a plain makes until he was off it. There had been grit under his boots for two days, and the rattle of shale; here there was a small dry sound at each step, and nothing came back from it. The floor was as smooth as the lid of a box, and it had no joint in it, and no bed; he had put his weight on such a thing on the ninety-sixth day, and had found that it was all one piece, and it was the same under him now for as far as he could have walked in any direction.
    14	
    15	*Thirty-one. Thirty-two.*
    16	
    17	There was more light under him than over. That was the thing that he could not get used to. The sky had gone the colour of slate, with the first stars in it, and the plain behind him was black; but the floor kept what the day had given it, and gave it up slowly. He could see his own boots, dark, going one before the other upon a pale ground. He threw no shadow. There was nothing above him bright enough to throw one.
    18	
    19	He did not look back. He had not looked back at the cut, and it had cost him something; there were five people standing at that rim, and a healer, who had each walked the length of a country to it. He had asked them to wait while he went in by himself.
    20	
    21	It was not that he wished to be alone, since he had been alone for a good part of his life, and had not cared for it. But every one of them would have left something at that cut which he would not. They would have walked beside him across this floor as people walk who have set down everything that they carry; and he would have gone among them with his hands full. He had not been able to face being the only one of six with a coat on.
    22	
    23	*Fifty.*
    24	
    25	The things that he carried were as they were on any ground.
    26	
    27	He went over them, as he might have gone over his pockets. There was a held breath in the soles of his feet, and a deep narrow thing under his breastbone, and a hot grain by the left hand. Something waited, through the whole of his frame, for a load that was worth sending down. There was a lid, which he kept shut. There were lanes, and a thin note, and a short edge; a slow limping current that knew its owner; a post, which had asked its two words at the cut and been told no. There was a rule folded on a shelf behind his eyes. And along him, from the heel to the palm, lay the newest, which had cracked two ribs for him the day before and was listening now to the hardest thing that it had ever stood upon.
    28	
    29	That made thirteen. Not one of them had so much as blinked at the line.
    30	
    31	They went round what they had always gone round. He did not go to look at that. He walked.
    32	
    33	*Seventy-eight. Seventy-nine.*
    34	
    35	His side had begun to speak, under the tape. He could not draw a whole breath, and so he counted upon the half of one.
    36	
    37	He thought, somewhere about the ninetieth stride, of a plate.
    38	
    39	He had stood on that when he was fourteen, with a great many people looking on. Every one of them had seen a sigil light for some child before him, and they were waiting to see his. The plate had lit late. He had stood there for the count of eleven with nothing happening, and had learned in those eleven what it is to be on a floor that was made for everybody else.
    40	
    41	He had been walking away from that plate ever since. He saw now, on a pale floor at the far end of the country, with five people waiting behind him at a cut, that he had also been walking toward this.
    42	
    43	*A hundred.*
    44	
    45	He stopped.
    46	
    47	---
    48	
    49	He went down on his knees, since a thing had to be done here, and he could not do it standing.
    50	
    51	It took him three movements, and the middle one was bad. He stayed for a little with his hands on his thighs, getting his breath. The grey was a foot from his face. He could see it by its own light, and there was nothing whatever to see: no stroke, and no grain. It was only pale, like the inside of a shell.
    52	
    53	He had turned the folded thing upon himself, the night before, on a shoulder of rock, and he had known then that he would do this as well. A reading that has been taken once is a hope; and he had the other half of the comparison lying under his knees, half a mile wide, where nobody would see his eyes afterward.
    54	
    55	He did it on purpose. He meant to write that down, since it was the first time that he had ever looked at a made thing with that tool and meant to; all the others had been taken from him between one breath and the next. He would count it. A thing that is chosen is counted.
    56	
    57	He let it come about.
    58	
    59	It leaned at the floor.
    60	
    61	*Still.*
    62	
    63	He had known that it would say so, and his heart struck once all the same.
    64	
    65	*Set.*
    66	
    67	It did not say *sits*, as it said of a page. It did not say *goes*, as it had said of every course of signs from the Fallow to a pier.
    68	
    69	*Bones.*
    70	
    71	Then it gave him the shape, with no more words, as it had given it in the dark.
    72	
    73	There was something that bore. It lay level under everything, whole, from one side of that place to the other, and nothing whatever was carried upon it. Here he was past the last of the rows, and there was not a mark. And it leaned. The whole bare floor leaned, as a line of cut signs leans, steadily; and he could not have said what it leaned at.
    74	
    75	It was the same reading.
    76	
    77	He knelt there and went over the two of them, as Karis would have laid two sheets side by side with the lamp low. He could find no difference. There was the order of the words, and the flatness of them; there was the thing beneath that carried nothing, and the lean, and no letter. If he had been handed both with his eyes shut, he could not have told which was a floor in a plain and which was a place under his own ribs.
    78	
    79	His eyes were shut, by then. The water had begun.
    80	
    81	It came faster than it ever had. He had looked for the length of five breaths, and he was being charged as though he had read a wall; and he thought, kneeling there with the heels of his hands in his eye-sockets, that it was a fair price. He had asked that tool for more in two nights than its owner would have asked of it in a year.
    82	
    83	When he could bear to open them, the floor had a ring round every part of it.
    84	
    85	He got up. That was worse than the kneeling.
    86	
    87	The star was gone. There was a smear where it had been, with a halo to it, among a great many other smears; he chose the lowest, and hoped. He had four hundred and six strides still to go, and he would have to walk them as a person crosses a room in the dark, with nothing to trust but the count.
    88	
    89	*A hundred and one.*
    90	
    91	He went on.
