# Coordinator-selected subset of plan-audit.md exact fixes (applied mechanically, 2026-10-12).
# ACCEPTED (25): the sixteen marker restorations (28-30, 32, 34, 38, 45-47, 52-57, 64), minimal and with the source's speakers; the two sign-nouns "bearer" (1, 36); the broken join (37); the three thaw intervals (31, 33, 35); the factor's seat for Vastin cut (13, 14, 74).
# AMENDED: 29 (the source's two halves are one continuous speech of Vastin's; no join); 33 ("It's a month gone." — the ice went on the 263rd-264th, thirty-three days before); 47 (the stake's return is NOT appended to Teague's scene-ending protected line; the author returns stake thirty-two to the tarred case in his own place); 53 (only "I'm a stop on my own route." is the source's wording; "three holds owed" is the packet's paraphrase and is given to the author as a fact); 57 (the source's stage direction after "It's his." is not imported).
# DECLINED:
#  2-12, 15-27, 58-63 (Vastin made to walk the whole road). The audit weighed the packet's "the road and the cut walked again on a bad knee" against Movement 3's ledger, but not against CLOSED Movement 6: after the alley (the 290th) the knee is twice its size; he is let down a ladder on a rope, sleeps in a stall, rides the handcart on the 291st-294th and takes a stair on his seat. Nineteen miles on the 295th is not available to that body. The author's answer stands: he rides a tail-board on the level at his own copper, gets down for every hill, walks the whole cut and stands on the leg there, and is put on the salt cart on the 298th by the field's first ruling. The packet's challenge is met by the hills and the cut.
#  39-44, 75-76 (the ages re-derived from a printed first-sheet date). A printed date would have to be subtracted from the present registry year, which is the one operation this book reserves for Cael's and Seln's unspoken sum. The author's corner figure stands as the device; the AUTHOR is to soften what it claims (a count of YEARS from the office's first sheet for the place, not proof of a sheet drawn in every year, and nothing about sheets kept back in a drawer).
#  48-51 (the Millrace count given to a "ford-keeper"). The woman with the net on the ford's stone is this edition's own LOCKED Book 7 (chapter 47: "Five off the ridge."); the author followed the edition, and no role is minted.
#  65-72 (the whole night exchange with Lira cut). Only "You went stiff" contradicts the page; the author re-grounds Lira's knowledge (see brief). The log, which the audit keeps and which Lira reads, already says that something unbidden came.
#  73 ("Twelve. And a mule." taken from Seln). Not protected speech; the plan pins the count only, and Seln's count here prepares his protected "Six. Six and no mule." at the book's end.
# {{JOIN}} marks a beat the AUTHOR writes (never the source's stage direction).

## Exact line fixes

1. `manuscript/chapter-41.md`
   old: `"The first of it's sound. It's the word you had on the face, for the mark." He did not say it as she would have said it; he said it as a man names a beam. "That's the bearer, and there's only the one. Two runs hang from it." The reed moved. "One there, of five. One there, of six, and that's the one in the thin. And after the pair of them, his number."`
   new: `"The first of it's sound. It's the word you had on the face, for the mark." He did not say it as she would have said it; he said it as a man names a beam. "That's what bears, and there's only the one. Two runs hang from it." The reed moved. "One there, of five. One there, of six, and that's the one in the thin. And after the pair of them, his number."`

2. `manuscript/chapter-43.md`
   old: `"Twenty, at each first frost, on the same bar, to the five that walk it before I let a wheel on. The long house's fire for the week that it takes, and the pot." The crook's foot came round, without haste, and pointed at the tail-board. "And for him, a seat at that fire for the whole of a cold term, every year that he cares to come, with nothing asked of him but to be within a shout of my road. I'll not say what for. I was on the lip."`
   new: `"Twenty, at each first frost, on the same bar, to the five that walk it before I let a wheel on. The long house's fire for the week that it takes, and the pot."`

3. `manuscript/chapter-43.md`
   old: `The old man had not stirred. He looked at the crook's foot as he had once looked at a milking stool, with grave attention, and it was plain that he did not mean to answer for a thing which had been put to another person's purse.`
   new: ``

4. `manuscript/chapter-43.md`
   old: `*Two hundred and ninety-sixth day. The halting-ground in the fold; thirteen miles. The cut walked east. Water in it, and no glass, and sheep on the top. Vastin walked it: seventeen of thirty. He stood on the leg once, with the stick off the ground, where the line had been. The factor offered twenty at each first frost, and a seat for the old man at the hold's fire. Brom said no. A hundred and twenty-eight.*`
   new: `*Two hundred and ninety-sixth day. The halting-ground in the fold; thirteen miles. The cut walked east. Water in it, and no glass, and sheep on the top. Vastin walked it: seventeen of thirty. He stood on the leg once, with the stick off the ground, where the line had been. The factor offered twenty at each first frost. Brom said no. A hundred and twenty-eight.*`

5. `manuscript/chapter-43.md`
   old: `[[TIER-B: "Here." — the packet gives the one word and pins no speaker. Staged at the place where the line was struck on the 279th, forty strides below the cut's head and three above Brom's flat stone, with the file halted on the dry edge and nothing on the ground to mark it. Vastin could own it (he has stopped there of himself and is looking at the road); Cael could own it (he is the nearest to him and is the only other who stood at that line). Nobody else is within four strides — coordinator to supply protected text]]`
   new: `Vastin said, "Here."`

6. `manuscript/chapter-43.md`
   old: `[[TIER-B: "Two words," … "Both of them ours." — elided, and the plan pins no speaker. Staged as the next thing said on the dry edge after the stick comes down, low, as the road allows at a halt; Cael's "Noted." follows it and answers it. Vastin could own it, speaking of what this road has taught him to say; so could Brom, who has turned on his stone three strides below and is looking up at the two of them — coordinator to supply protected text]]`
   new: `"Two words," Vastin said. "Both of them ours."`

7. `manuscript/chapter-44.md`
   old: `[[TIER-B: "Ten of us on a wall's count. Your six and my four." … "Contract. Not more than that. I'll not have it called more." — elided between its two parts; the plan pins the term on Teague and gives the heads (ten on a wall's count; shares by count; the field Teague's; the reading Karis's) without their wording. Staged as Teague's, said to Cael at the bench's end before the whole room, with his eyes having just gone to Vastin at the door-post. The four heads follow below in my own words, as his, so that the restored unit may stand before them or round them — coordinator to supply protected text]]`
   new: `Teague said, "Ten of us on a wall's count. Your six and my four."\n\n{{JOIN}}\n\nTeague said, "Contract. Not more than that. I'll not have it called more."`

8. `manuscript/chapter-44.md`
   old: `There was no frost to speak of; the thaw was three weeks old, and the joint had not talked since the board came off. He brought it all the same, wrung out of the kettle, on his two palms, as he had on every night since he joined. Teague held out the arm; he had tried to tell him once that it was not wanted, and had seen the boy's face, and had let it be wanted.`
